// The library a program has when it mentions `Thread` or `ScopedThread` and declares neither itself: a closure started on
// a thread of its own and joined for its result.
//
// A Thread shares nothing with the program that starts it: the closure owns what it captured (a closure or a result that
// holds a reference is refused where the copy of spawn is made, MirMoves.checkSpawns). A ScopedThread is lent its
// closure, which may borrow, and only reads through it: the handle holds the loan of the closure (`from g`), so what the
// closure borrows is not written until the handle is joined, and a closure that can update through a reference, or a
// result that holds one, is refused the same way. Either way running the closure where it is started gives the values
// that running it on another thread gives, and that is how the validator and `tersus eval` read it; native code runs it
// on a thread of its own (MirThreads). A caller knows of the result what the closure's bound says, which is nothing: a
// fact it needs comes through the result's type, a struct with an invariant. Both handles are linear, so every thread is
// joined, once.

linear struct Thread<T> {
    value: T,
};

impl<T> Thread<T> {
    fn spawn<F: FnOnce() -> T>(f: F) -> Self [{ }] [{ }] {
        return Self { value: f() };
    };

    fn join(self: Self) -> T [{ }] [{ affirm return = self.value; }] {
        return self.value;
    };
};

linear struct ScopedThread<T, F> {
    value: T,
    f: &F,
};

impl<T, F: Fn() -> T> ScopedThread<T, F> {
    fn spawn(g: &F) -> Self from g [{ }] [{ }] {
        return Self { value: g(), f: &g };
    };

    fn join(self: Self) -> T [{ }] [{ }] {
        return self.value;
    };
};
